    answer:
      "<p>No universal answer. Depends on your body and work requirements. Early birds do well with five thirty to six thirty. Night owls need seven thirty to eight thirty. Flexible schedules mean wake time matters less than consistency. Key is same time every day within thirty minutes. Even weekends.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Should I check email first?",
    answer:
      "<p>No. Checking email hands your agenda to others. Complete routine first. Spend first work hour on your most important task. Schedule email checks for mid-morning and afternoon. Exception: time-sensitive roles can do a quick emergency-only scan. Emails from eleven PM can wait until ten AM.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Morning routine with kids?",
    answer:
      "<p>Requires creative timing. Wake before them if possible. Even thirty minutes of quiet helps. Involve them in parts. Family breakfast. Kid-friendly stretching. Parallel routines. They do theirs while you do yours. Adjust expectations. Fifteen minutes beats nothing. Focus on the transition element. Short ritual that signals work time still helps.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Not a morning person?",
    answer:
      "<p>Night owls can still benefit from routines. Don't fight biology. If you're alert at ten, design around that. Give yourself sixty to ninety minutes from wake to work. Light exposure after waking helps. Consistency matters more than timing. Same eight AM routine every day is fine.</p>",
  },
  {
